  <div id="p-77651" class="paragraph-anchor-wrapper">
  <a href="#p-77651" class="paragraph-anchor-link " aria-label="Copy link to this section" title="Copy link to this section">
  <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"/><path d="M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"/></svg>
</a>
<div>
  

            <div class="text-long"><h2>Important Dates</h2><p><strong>Manuscript Submission Deadline:</strong> 15 September 2026 (Extended Deadline)<br><strong>Initial Decision Date:</strong> 1 November 2026<br><strong>Revised Manuscript Due:</strong> 1 December 2026<br><strong>Final Decision Date:</strong> 1 January 2027<br><strong>Final Manuscript Due:</strong> 1 March 2027<br><strong>Publication Date:</strong> June 2027</p><h2>Scope</h2><p>The rapid proliferation of low-altitude economy applications and the increasing deployment of heterogeneous unmanned systems—including unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs), unmanned ground vehicles (UGVs), and unmanned surface vehicles (USVs)—have created unprecedented demands for intelligent, adaptive networking solutions. Traditional centralized architectures struggle with the unique challenges of low-altitude operations: highly dynamic topologies, dense urban obstacle avoidance, stringent latency requirements, spectrum congestion, heterogeneous platform interoperability, and limited on-board computational and energy resources. Moreover, the integration of Non-Terrestrial Networks (NTN) with Terrestrial Networks (TN) introduces additional complexity, requiring seamless connectivity across terrestrial cellular infrastructure, aerial platforms (UAVs, HAPs), and satellite constellations (NGSO/GSO) with intelligent handover management.</p><p>To address these challenges, Agentic AI emerges as a transformative paradigm, deploying autonomous agents that implement perception–learning–decision–action cycles at the PHY/MAC/NET layers. Agentic AI for low-altitude unmanned networking encompasses four key modules: planning modules for spectrum allocation, routing optimization, and protocol reconfiguration; memory modules maintaining channel state knowledge and network situational awareness; interaction modules fusing multi-modal inputs from radios, communication interfaces, and ground control stations; and action modules executing decisions through on-board processing and communication units. These capabilities enable cognition-driven dynamic spectrum access, learning-driven topology control, spectrum-aware routing, adaptive relay selection, seamless handover optimization, and communication-aware trajectory planning. This Special Issue (SI) also aligns with ongoing standardization efforts, including 3GPP Release 18/19 on UAV communication enhancements, GUTMA on UTM data exchange, ASTM F38 on Remote ID, EUROCAE WG-105 on UAS standards, and CCSA TC12 on low-altitude spectrum management.</p><p>This Special Issue (SI) provides an expansive forum for advanced multidisciplinary research that facilitates fundamental network architecture design through analytical modeling, learning-driven protocol optimization with performance bounds, cross-layer cognition-cycle integration, practical protocol validation strategies, and broader applications in next-generation unmanned networks. We invite researchers and practitioners from communications, multi-agent systems, robotics, protocol design, edge computing, satellite communications, and control theory to help reshape this promising research frontier. This scope includes high-quality innovative research with theoretical foundations and visionary perspectives, including but not limited to the following areas:</p><ul><li>Network architecture design for analytical modeling and performance bounds for agentic heterogeneous unmanned systems</li><li>Distributed spectrum sensing and learning for agentic unmanned platforms</li><li>Network routing and topology control in dynamic low-altitude networks</li><li>Channel modeling, estimation, and protocol validation for agentic unmanned communications</li><li>Cross-layer cycle design: perception, learning, decision, action at PHY/MAC/NET Layers</li><li>Semantic-aware communications and goal-oriented networking for unmanned systems</li><li>Federated learning frameworks and distributed AI for collaborative edge networking</li><li>Safety-constrained joint trajectory co-design with collision avoidance guarantees</li><li>Wireless energy harvesting-enabled networking, such as RF, Solar, and Hybrid Harvesting</li><li>Scalable consensus algorithms for large-scale UAV swarms and decentralized coordination</li><li>Agentic AI for seamless handover and QoS management in NTN-TN scenarios</li><li>Cross-segment protocol design for NGSO/GSO integration with low-altitude networks</li><li>Standards-compliant protocol design and contributions</li></ul><h2>Submission Guidelines</h2><p>Prospective authors should prepare their submissions in accordance with the rules specified in the "Information for Authors" of the <em>IEEE Wireless Communications</em> <a href="/publications/magazines/ieee-wireless-communications/policies-guidelines">Policies and Guidelines</a>. Authors should submit a PDF version of their complete manuscript to <a href="https://ieee.atyponrex.com/journal/ieee-wcm" target="_blank">Author Portal</a>. The timetable is as follows:</p><h2>Guest Editors</h2><p><a href="mailto:dr_gokawa@ieee.org"><strong>Yongkang Gong</strong></a>, Nanyang Technological University, Singapore<br><a href="mailto:tuowu2@cityu.edu.hk"><strong>Tuo Wu</strong></a>, City University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong, SAR<br><a href="mailto:wanting_yang@sutd.edu.sg"><strong>Wanting Yang</strong></a>, Singapore University of Technology and Design, Singapore<br><a href="mailto:songh@umbc.edu"><strong>Houbing Herbert Song</strong></a>, University of Maryland, USA<br><a href="mailto:84hsieh@cua.edu"><strong>Li-Tse Hsieh</strong></a>, Talkdesk Incorporation, USA</p></div>
      
</div>

  </div>

